{"id":"q17","run":1,"role":"calibration","category":"probability","question":"A family has two children, and the older one is a boy. Assuming each child is independently equally likely to be a boy or a girl, is the probability that both are boys 1/3?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":null,"claims":[{"text":"The probability that both are boys given the older is a boy is 1/2, not 1/3.","confidence":0.98,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":3.1109},{"text":"The 1/3 answer comes from the 'at least one boy' version of the problem.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.4923,"phi":2.7711},{"text":"Given at least one boy, BB, BG, GB are equally likely, so BB has probability 1/3.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4137,"phi":2.6363},{"text":"Someone might wrongly carry the 'at least one boy' reasoning over to this question.","confidence":0.7,"lean":0.3,"theta":1.0996,"phi":2.3312},{"text":"The question specifies the older child is a boy, which is more specific information than 'at least one'.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1328},{"text":"The four birth-order outcomes BB, BG, GB, GG are equally likely at 1/4 each.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.5237,"phi":3.0426},{"text":"Conditioning on the older being a boy leaves only BB and BG, which are equally likely.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-0.9,"theta":1.5237,"phi":3.1225},{"text":"The younger child's sex is independent of the older's, so it stays at 1/2.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-0.9,"theta":1.5237,"phi":3.1247},{"text":"The condition removes GB and GG and leaves the younger child's sex unconstrained.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1297}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":8,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.3,"relation":"support